The relationship between '''[[Okaiken]] and [[Wolffrea]]''' is among the coldest bilateral relationships maintained by either state. The two countries have exchanged no resident ambassadors since 1976, conduct what little business they have through protecting powers, and describe each other in language normally reserved for adversaries — while formally remaining at peace. The relationship began as one of the most profitable of Okaiken's early postwar partnerships, curdled through five decades of sanctions, seizures, and recrimination, and by 2026 consisted almost entirely of things each government does ''to'' the other: maritime interdictions, asset freezes, démarches, and votes in the [[Adonian Assembly]].

The relationship is structured by an irony neither side enjoys acknowledging. Okaiken, a defeated and occupied power reintegrated into international life through the [[Adonian Community]], built its postwar reconstruction partly on the steel of a neutral state that had sat out the [[Great Adonian War]] and grown rich on the proceeds; Wolffrea, a settler republic that constitutionalized racial separation, spent the same decades citing Okaiken's own history of ethnic stratification to deflect Sanese criticism of [[Rassentrennung]]. Each government considers the other's moral posture fraudulent, and the mutual conviction of hypocrisy — more than any concrete dispute — is what gives the relationship its edge.

==History==

===First contacts and the postwar trade era (1907–1968)===

Formal contact between the Sanese archipelago and Wolffrea predates both modern states. The [[Mitoyagi Empire]] opened a consulate-general in [[Blumenaue]] in 1907, primarily to service Sanese merchant shipping calling at the Pfein Sea ports; the consulate closed with the outbreak of the [[Great Adonian War]], in which Wolffrea remained neutral throughout. Relations resumed in 1953 under the postwar settlement, when Okaiken — rebuilding under the armistice — found in neutral Wolffrea's idle industrial capacity exactly what its reconstruction required. Between 1953 and 1955, Wolffrean steel and rail plate supplied a substantial share of the material for Okaiken's railway reconstruction, a fact that Sanese historians of the period describe with more discomfort than either government has ever mustered.

Both states were members of the Adonian Community from the early 1950s, and the relationship flourished within it. Embassies were exchanged in 1961, and bilateral trade peaked in 1964 at roughly I$340 million — Okaiken exporting ships, rolling stock, and early electronics; Wolffrea exporting chromium, tungsten, and industrial minerals from the plantation and mining economy of its northwest. A Sanese trading community of several hundred formed in Blumenaue, and Wolffrean machinery firms maintained agencies in [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. Internal MoFA assessments from 1963, declassified decades later, warned that the consolidation of the [[State Mutualism|State Mutualist]] party-state made the partnership a political liability; the trade ministry's assessment, which carried the day, described the same facts as "a competitive advantage."

===Suspension from the Adonian Community (1968)===

In 1968 the [[Adonian Assembly]], responding to the constitutional entrenchment of [[Rassentrennung]], rejected the credentials of the Blumenaue delegation and indefinitely suspended Wolffrea's voting rights and representation, declaring the State Mutualist regime an illegitimate pariah subject to comprehensive sanctions — a package the diplomatic correspondence of the era came to call a "diplomatic quarantine." Okaiken voted for the suspension. The remedy mattered more in law than in rhetoric: Wolffrea was not deleted from the international architecture but suspended within it, remaining subject to the AC Charter and the jurisdiction of the AC Court of Justice — the technicality on which the 2026 red notices against the Wolffrean leadership would later rest. The vote was not as unanimous within the Sanese government as it was in the Assembly. The {{Nihongo|Ushijima cable|牛島電文|Ushijima denpon}} — a confidential 1968 memorandum by Deputy Foreign Minister {{Nihongo|Ushijima Renzō|牛島蓮三}} — argued that sanctions would "purchase moral standing at the price of Okaiken's recovery," that they would fall on the Black Wolffrean majority rather than the settler state that had disenfranchised them, and that censure plus targeted measures would serve both principle and interest. The Foreign Minister of the day overruled it in a single paragraph. The cable remained classified for thirty-five years and has been a fixture of the relationship ever since its release: Wolffrean state media cites it as proof that Okaiken's conscience was always for sale, while Sanese commentators cite it as proof that the government knew the sanctions regime would fail and voted for it anyway.

===Sanctions and the Pfein circuit (1975–2002)===

[[Adonian Community Resolution 842]] imposed the first comprehensive AC sanctions regime on Wolffrea in 1975. Okaiken complied formally and quickly — the Blumenaue embassy closed in 1975, the Wolffrean mission in Okami in 1976 — and then, for four years, complied in practice rather less than formally. The {{Nihongo|Pfein circuit|パファイン回路|Pafain kairo}}, exposed in 1979 by the Sakaki Committee of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]], documented how two Sanese trading houses — Kurogane Sangyō and Tsubame Trading — moved Wolffrean chromium through misdeclared third-country transshipments, with working capital quietly arranged by two Okami banks. The Diet committee's report ended both firms. No bank officer was ever prosecuted; the banks settled administratively in terms sealed until 2003. The circuit's profits were modest and its costs were not: Wolffrea took it as proof that the sanctions were theater, and Okaiken took it as a national embarrassment that, in the words of one retired ambassador, "cost more in standing than it ever paid in profit."

The two states have traded almost nothing legally since 1975, and the relationship became a ledger of enforcement. From 2002, following [[Adonian Community Resolution 1392]] and the growth of the Wolffrean state-sponsored drug economy, that enforcement turned kinetic. Sanese Coast Guard and customs units began seizing cartel shipments moving through East Fosian waters and container ports; cumulative seizures reached 6.2 tonnes by 2021, the largest single case being 1.1 tonnes found aboard a misdeclared machinery consignment at Okami in 2013. That same year, a cartel mothership rammed the Coast Guard patrol vessel ''Shimagane'' in international waters south of the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] approaches; the fourteen Wolffrean nationals aboard were arrested, tried in the Okami District Court, and served full sentences over Blumenaue's public demand for their release as "hostages of a sanctions regime." Wolffrean state media has described Sanese interdiction as "piracy with paperwork" ever since; the Coast Guard describes it as the only chapter of the relationship in which the paperwork is honest.

===The quiet channel (1986–1994)===

During the [[Wolffrean Bush War|Bush War]], at the request of the AC Secretariat, Okaiken quietly relayed messages between Blumenaue and Areza through its embassy in Yaweka — ceasefire feelers, prisoner-exchange mechanics, and humanitarian-access arrangements. The channel was never acknowledged by either government, and Okaiken also used its standing in the sanctions reviews of the era to press quietly for humanitarian carve-outs. Wolffrean officials of the period regarded Okami as "the only member that listened." The gratitude did not survive the channel's exposure: when Wolffrean intelligence publicized the arrangement in 2003, alongside the Ushijima cable and the Pfein circuit settlements, it did so to characterize Okaiken as a state that "lectured in the Assembly and passed notes in the corridor." MoFA's private assessment was blunter than its public one: the channel had produced three prisoner exchanges and no gratitude.

===Wolffrean War (2021–present)===

{{Main|Wolffrean War}}

Okaiken voted for [[Adonian Community Resolution 1434]] in 2022, expanding sanctions to individuals and entities supporting the State Mutualist war effort, and has since treated the relationship as a sanctions-management file rather than a diplomatic one. The OICA has disbursed roughly I$180 million in humanitarian assistance to the frontline host states — [[Alwana]], [[Tategazi]], [[Yaweka]], and [[Bonande]] — while the last Sanese nationals registered in Wolffrea, remnants of the old trading community, were evacuated by 2023. MoFA's Wolffrea desk, by its own internal description, now exists "to implement Assembly resolutions, protect the evacuation corridor, and say nothing quotable."

==Mzinti attack and Resolution 4934==
{{Main|Mzinti attack|Adonian Community Resolution 4934}}

The [[Mzinti attack]] — the delivery of VX nerve agent against [[Front for the Liberation of Ukumfenguza|FLU]] positions on the night of June 3–4, 2026, which killed 214 fighters and 37 civilians — produced one of the few unanimous votes of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet|Ikazaki era]]: a Diet resolution of condemnation on June 12, adopted without dissent. Foreign Minister [[Kenichi Mori]] told the House that "a state that gases the people it refuses to count as citizens has forfeited the right to be called a state; it has not forfeited the obligation to answer for it."

Okaiken voted in favor of [[Adonian Community Resolution 4934]] on September 25, 2026, and implemented the resulting red notices within a week, freezing I$12.4 million in Okami waterfront property held through nominees associated with the Ehrhardt family, a State Mutualist elite lineage — the first use of Okaiken's 2022 targeted-sanctions implementing statute against a Wolffrean interest. Blumenaue's response was personal. Staatsrat [[Merten Kruse-Essert]] described the notices as "a lynch mob with letterhead" and Okaiken as "the vending machine of the Assembly — insert resolution, receive abstention," reviving a Wolffrean press nickname for Okaiken that dates from the sanctions reviews of the 1990s.

==Refugee policy and the Toribe memorandum==

The most damaging self-inflicted wound in the relationship's recent history was domestic. Between 2021 and 2026 Okaiken admitted roughly 4,100 Wolffrean refugees: about 3,600 under skilled-migrant visas — overwhelmingly White Wolffrean professionals of the settler minority, whose qualifications, savings, and documentation survived the collapse in ways the majority population's did not — and about 500 under the humanitarian quota — a White Wolffrean share well above even the demographics of the exodus itself, which refugee agencies put at 55 to 70 per cent. In June 2026 the {{Nihongo|Toribe memorandum|鳥部覚書|Toribe oboegaki}}, an internal Immigration Bureau guidance note, was leaked to the Sanese press; it described "cultural compatibility" screening for Wolffrean applicants in terms that refugee advocacy organizations called "a points system that measures the color of a crisis." The FLU's political office, in a statement widely reprinted, drew the conclusion the government had avoided: "The wealthy of the regime's own community board aircraft for Areza. The majority we fight for are offered sympathy at a distance."

The Diet debate that followed produced an unusual coalition of backbenchers from the governing parties and the opposition, and Mori announced in July 2026 that the humanitarian quota would rise from 300 to 3,000 per year and that compatibility screening would end. Wolffrean state media covered the affair with visible satisfaction, as proof that "even the sanctions aristocracy admits its doors were built for one kind of refugee" — a rare instance on record of Blumenaue and its critics agreeing on the facts of the matter, if nothing else.

==Pfein Sea evacuation programme (2026)==

When the Adonian Community launched the Pfein Sea evacuation programme in September 2026 — a six-year AC-protected sealift intended to move the displaced at a rate of roughly 10,000 a day — some 2.3 million a year against a displaced population that by the autumn exceeded seventeen million — the [[Wolffrean refugee crisis]] — of whom 14.7 million were outside the country and 3.1 million were displaced within it — Okaiken made its largest commitment to an AC operation since the peacekeeping deployments of the 1990s: two destroyers of the SIF's Escort Flotilla, including the ''ISN Shirakumo'', and a replenishment ship under AC operational command, the first Sanese combat-zone deployment since the Great Adonian War. The enabling vote in the House of Commoners passed with opposition support after the SDP abstained in cabinet, and eleven Sanese-flagged merchant hulls were chartered into the lift. When Wolffrea announced on September 26 that AC ships could no longer dock at ports under its control, the escort group withdrew with the AC operation to Alwana, where it has remained since. Okaiken is among the largest assessed contributors to the hosting payments authorized under [[Adonian Community Resolution 4935]] — the government confirmed it would meet its assessed share in full — most of which fell on Alwana, where 5.2 million refugees strained services far past the breaking point. The sealift was triage rather than rescue: Sindewand and Bonande refused entry to White Wolffreans, and the displaced who could not board a ship stayed in the camps where the war found them.

==October 2026 crisis==

The October escalation placed the relationship under more strain than any event since the 1968 credential suspension. After media reports of attacks on refugees on October 2, Mori summoned the Wolffrean interests section through the protecting power — a formality, since summoning is what the relationship is for — and Okaiken voted for [[Adonian Community Resolution 4938]], sanctioning Bonande for its unilateral seizure of a Wolffrean port on October 10; Mori told the Assembly that multilateral procedure and the rule of law could not be abandoned, remarking that "humanitarian corridors cannot be built on unilateral gunboat diplomacy." Okaiken abstained on Resolution 4936 (the proposed sanctions against Sindewand), lobbying privately for refugee transit instead, abstained on Resolution 4937, voted against Resolution 4939, and abstained on [[Adonian Community Resolution 4940]], which authorized the coalition intervention conducted outside AC joint command with AC observers. Okaiken voted for [[Adonian Community Resolution 4941]], which suspended Bonande and Yaweka from the Community on October 19. Mori's formulation of the government's position — "Okaiken will not vote for a war it does not intend to fight; an abstention is not neutrality, it is a receipt" — was received badly in both directions: coalition states read it as free-riding, and Blumenaue's state press ran it under the headline “THE RECEIPT ARRIVES.”

The resolution did not resolve the crisis that had prompted it. Refugee organizations concluded that 4940 had cleared the sea lanes for warships while leaving the seventeen million displaced where they were — in camps behind closed borders, on the wrong side of an arithmetic of ten thousand a day.

On October 16, the ro-ro ferry ''Aozora Maru'', Sanese-flagged and chartered to the AC lift, was struck by shore fire during the closure of the bay, injuring three crew members. Okaiken responded with what the press called the "double démarche": the Bonandian ambassador was summoned over the indiscriminate firing on evacuation shipping, and a protest was lodged with Blumenaue, through Breisland, over the missile barrage then in its first week. Behind the démarches, the channel that shaped the crisis was quieter and more compromising: Okaiken passed evacuation-corridor deconfliction coordinates to the Joint Working Group for Peace in Northern Kaftia command at [[Joint Base Ajowa]] ''and'', through the same channel, to Blumenaue, so that refugee shipping would stay off both sides' target lists. The arrangement leaked on October 20. Kruse-Essert told the state press that Okaiken had been "drawing the target map" for the invasion; MoFA replied that the coordinates protected ships carrying civilians, "and the government that gassed Mzinti knew precisely what they were."

==Diplomatic representation==

There have been no resident missions since 1976. The interests of both states are represented by [[Breisland]], the former colonial power, which retains a consulate in Blumenaue. Okami has long suspected the Breisland mission of serving as Blumenaue's listening post in the capital, while Blumenaue describes the arrangement as "colonial custody by another name." Consular services are limited to emergency protection of the few remaining nationals on either side.

==Public opinion and cultural relations==

A Sanese Broadcasting Association poll conducted in October 2026 found 87 percent of respondents holding an unfavorable view of Wolffrea, the highest negative rating recorded for any country in the series' history; Wolffrean state media's standing description of Okaiken, "the sanctions aristocracy," has appeared in its editorials for over two decades. Institutional academic ties were cut under the boycott of 2002, though the Blumenaue–Okami Comparative Linguistics Exchange survived until 2019 as the last formal scholarly link between the two countries. The two states have never fielded teams against each other in any AC-sanctioned championship: Wolffrea has been excluded from international sport since 1968.

Wolffrean propaganda's favorite needle is domestic: that Okaiken "spent a century deciding who counted as Sanese" and is in no position to lecture anyone on racial hierarchy. MoFA's standing reply is that the comparison is a false equivalence — the Atsu and Kajika peoples hold citizenship, representation, and recognized language status, while Rassentrennung denies citizenship to the majority of the population — and that "a state that requires this distinction to be explained has already conceded the argument." Most Sanese historians support the government's facts while noting, as one put it, that the reply is the kind that wins debates and changes no minds in Blumenaue.

